::ENG2_U06_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which perspective is most focused for a presentation to school leaders?{
=A. A supervised after-school study space would help students work safely while waiting on campus.#Correct. This is clear, focused, and audience-appropriate.
~B. The library has books, snacks are good, and hallways exist.#Incorrect. This mixes unrelated ideas.
~C. I like many things about school.#Incorrect. This is too broad and personal.
~D. Students do homework sometimes and also read novels.#Incorrect. This is not a focused perspective.
}

::ENG2_U06_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] Why should a speaker remove a detail about favorite library books from this presentation?{
~A. Books are always bad evidence.#Incorrect. Books can be relevant in other presentations.
=B. It does not directly support the purpose of explaining after-school study space.#Correct. The detail distracts from the presentation's purpose.
~C. It uses too many numbers.#Incorrect. The detail does not use numbers.
~D. It is the strongest safety evidence.#Incorrect. It does not address safety or study space.
}

::ENG2_U06_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which detail best supports the argument for study space?{
~A. Some students enjoy mystery books.#Incorrect. This does not support the argument.
~B. Cafeteria snacks are popular.#Incorrect. This distracts from the argument.
=C. Students who wait after school often work in noisy hallways.#Correct. This supports the need for a quiet study space.
~D. The school has many clubs.#Incorrect. This is not directly related to the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase uses academic vocabulary that fits the lesson?{
~A. random stuff#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. cool things#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. whatever details#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. coherent focus#Correct. This academic phrase matches the lesson skill.
}

::ENG2_U06_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which checklist best supports coherent focus?{
~A. Add every detail, even if unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated details weaken focus.
=B. State one perspective, choose relevant details, remove distractions, and use precise vocabulary.#Correct. This checklist supports a focused oral presentation.
~C. Begin with snacks and end with a different topic.#Incorrect. This creates confusion.
~D. Avoid stating the main point.#Incorrect. The perspective must be clear.
}
